Notebook[{Cell["Generic unequal-charge Wald--Tachikawa normalization", 
   "Title"], Cell["Version-3 human notation guide. The archived charge calculation is unchanged; the independent orientation guard added below does not modify it. In the compact solution and verifier notebooks: \
x=r^2; q1,q2,q3 are the harmonic parameters mu1,mu2,mu3, not normalized \
conserved charges; jrot is the rotation parameter j; U1,U2,U3 are the \
electric corrections delta u_I; B1,B2,B3 are the gauge-covariant fibre \
corrections; Om is delta omega; Ft, Gr, and Sq are the temporal warp, radial \
warp, and squashing corrections; P and S are delta p and delta s, where \
X1=exp(2p/3-s), X2=exp(2p/3+s), and X3=exp(-4p/3). Physical quantities are \
Q_I=Pi mu_I/(4 G5) and J1=J2=Pi j/(4 G5). See the report section \
'Human-readable notation and result at a glance' for the full dictionary.", 
   "Text"], 
  Cell["Self-contained curvature, mixed ARR, Komar, and asymptotic-tail \
audit", "Subtitle"], Cell["This notebook evaluates the generic angular charge \
at spatial infinity for the active lambda3=(0,0,1) standard-Weyl invariant. \
It embeds the exact background tensors, reconstructs the elementary ansatz \
tensors, uses R_mn{}^(cd) with the first Riemann pair lowered in the mixed \
A^3 wedge R wedge R term, and projects both double two-forms onto the \
algebraic Riemann symmetry class. No external file is read.", "Text"], 
  Cell["The result is first order in lambda3, exact in jrot, symbolic in \
q1,q2,q3, and assumes positive charges, asymptotic flatness, a nonrotating \
frame at infinity, and A_psi^I tending to zero.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Levi--Civita and chart orientation. The flat-index symbol in the oriented frame has epsilonLower[0,1,2,3,4]=+1 and epsilonUpper[0,1,2,3,4]=-1. The curved tensor is its five-vielbein transform and includes the determinant. The BMPV base orientation is vol4=-(r^3/8) dr wedge sigma1 wedge sigma2 wedge sigma3, so (t,r,theta,phi,psi) is orientation reversing. Consequently the curved coordinate components are epsilonLower=-Sqrt[-g] epsilonTilde and epsilonUpper=+epsilonTilde/Sqrt[-g], where the raw positive array used below is the alternating density epsilonTilde.", "Text"],
